For eleven years, the clock on the Harmon County Courthouse showed the same time: 4:17. Residents {{1|had long since stopped expecting it to move, they}} gave directions by it ("turn left when the clock says quarter past four") and joked that the county had finally found a way to stop time. {{2|The courthouse itself was built in 1902 from local limestone.}}

Ellison found the mechanism in worse shape than she {{3|expected. Pigeons}} had nested in the gear housing, and decades of grease had hardened into something closer to tar. {{4|Ellison, however, had spent thirty years repairing factory equipment, and she was not discouraged.}} She removed each gear, {{5|soaked it overnight in solvent, and scrubbing}} the teeth with a brass brush.

The hardest problem was a part worn beyond repair: a small toothed wheel, called the escapement wheel, {{6|that release}} the clock's stored energy in measured steps. {{7|Escapement wheels were first used in European clocks centuries ago.}} Ellison {{8|admired}} the worn edges of the broken original, calculated the angle of each tooth, and cut a new wheel by hand over the course of a winter.

On a Saturday morning in June, Ellison set the hands to the correct time and released the pendulum. The clock {{9|began to tick, and it was ticking}}. A small crowd had gathered on the courthouse lawn, and when the minute hand moved for the first time in eleven years, somebody started clapping. {{10|Within a week, a few residents admitted they no longer knew where to turn left.}}

When the Westbrook Public Library added a seed library in 2019, the librarians were not sure anyone would use it. The idea was simple: patrons could {{1|check up}} a packet of seeds in spring, grow the plants, and return seeds saved from the harvest in the fall. {{2|The library's first catalog listed forty varieties, most of them donated by a single retired farmer.}}

The first spring, only a dozen patrons borrowed seeds. {{3|Therefore,}} by the next year, word had spread through the town's garden club and its two elementary schools, and more than three hundred packets went out. Both {{4|schools science teachers}}, in particular, found that a packet of bean seeds could anchor a whole unit on plant life cycles. Students who planted the seeds at home {{5|bringing}} their harvests back to class in September.

Not every packet came back as hoped. Gardeners discovered that some vegetables, {{6|such as squash, cross-pollinate}} easily, so seeds saved from them may not grow true to the parent plant. {{7|Seeds from these plants can produce offspring that are different from the plants they came from.}} The librarians responded by labeling each packet "easy," "medium," or "advanced" to save, and the farmer's beans, which reliably breed true, became the most popular "easy" choice.

Today the seed library circulates more than a thousand packets a year. {{8|It's shelves}} hold heirloom tomatoes, drought-tolerant peppers, and {{9|a variety of corn which was grown by the founders of the town and which had nearly been lost entirely}}. {{10|No one at the library still wonders whether patrons will use the seeds.}}
